WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.200
Okay.

00:04.240 --> 00:05.680
So what is exactly grouped by.

00:05.720 --> 00:08.840
It's going to go and combine the rows with the same value.

00:08.840 --> 00:11.040
So it's going to go and combine and smash.

00:11.080 --> 00:14.520
Press your rows to make it aggregated and more combined.

00:14.520 --> 00:18.920
So all that groupby does, it aggregates a column by another column.

00:18.920 --> 00:24.680
Like for example, if you want to find the total score by country, so you aggregate all the scores

00:24.680 --> 00:26.800
value for one country.

00:26.800 --> 00:29.760
If you have this kind of tasks, then you can use the groupby.

00:29.800 --> 00:31.320
Let's see the syntax of that.

00:31.360 --> 00:33.280
We will start as usual with the select.

00:33.280 --> 00:35.960
And now what we want to see in the result is two columns.

00:35.960 --> 00:39.200
So we have to specify like a category like the country.

00:39.200 --> 00:41.920
This is the value that you want to group the data by.

00:41.920 --> 00:44.360
And another one where you are doing the aggregations.

00:44.360 --> 00:47.200
So for example you are saying I would like to see the total score.

00:47.200 --> 00:50.840
So we use the function sum in order to summarize the values of the score.

00:50.880 --> 00:55.600
After that, as usual, we use the from in order to select the data from a specific table.

00:55.600 --> 00:58.720
And now comes the magic we use after the from group.

00:58.760 --> 01:00.200
By now SQL understands.

01:00.240 --> 01:02.680
Okay, I have now to combine the data I.

01:02.680 --> 01:07.160
I have to group up the data by something, and this time we are saying you have to group up the data

01:07.160 --> 01:08.080
by the country.

01:08.080 --> 01:14.360
So that means each value of the country must be presented in the output only once and for each country

01:14.360 --> 01:18.040
we want to see the aggregation and that is the total score.

01:18.040 --> 01:19.760
So let's see how SQL can execute it.

01:19.760 --> 01:21.240
So it can first start with the from.

01:21.240 --> 01:23.400
It can go and get the data from the database.

01:23.400 --> 01:25.240
And then it's going to execute the group by.

01:25.280 --> 01:29.320
And now SQL understand okay I have to group up now the data by their country.

01:29.320 --> 01:32.560
And it understands it has to aggregate the scores for that.

01:32.560 --> 01:37.040
So it's going to go and identify the rows that are sharing the same value.

01:37.080 --> 01:41.160
Like for example here we have two rows for Germany and it can bring it to the results.

01:41.160 --> 01:43.760
So now we have two rows for the same country.

01:43.800 --> 01:46.080
But since we are saying group by country.

01:46.160 --> 01:52.120
SQL can try and combine them, smash them together in only one row, so each value of the country must

01:52.120 --> 01:54.280
exist at maximum once.

01:54.280 --> 01:55.440
We cannot leave it like this.

01:55.440 --> 01:56.800
So now what we're going to do with the scores?

01:56.800 --> 01:57.760
We have two scores.

01:57.800 --> 02:00.080
Now SQL is going to check the aggregate function.

02:00.080 --> 02:01.520
It is the summarization.

02:01.520 --> 02:05.390
So and it's going to go and add those values 350 plus 500.

02:05.430 --> 02:08.710
And with that, we're going to get the total score of 850.

02:08.750 --> 02:13.030
And with that, as you can see SQL is combining those two rows into one.

02:13.030 --> 02:15.510
So in the output Germany will exist only one.

02:15.510 --> 02:18.230
And above the scores we will get the total score.

02:18.230 --> 02:21.110
And the same thing can happen for the next value in the country.

02:21.110 --> 02:23.230
We have the USA, we have it twice.

02:23.230 --> 02:24.710
So we're going to get two rows.

02:24.870 --> 02:29.870
And SQL is going to combine those two rows and one, because USA must exist only once.

02:29.910 --> 02:32.190
And with the scores we will have the total scores.

02:32.190 --> 02:34.990
So 900 plus zero we will get 900.

02:35.030 --> 02:38.510
And with that SQL converted those two rows into one.

02:38.510 --> 02:42.590
And for the last value in the countries we have the UK, it's going to stay as it is.

02:42.630 --> 02:46.710
There is no need to smash and combine anything because it's already one value.

02:46.710 --> 02:52.670
So my friends, if you are looking to the output, you can see we grouped the original data by the country

02:52.670 --> 02:57.190
and that means we're going to get one row for each value inside the country column.

02:57.230 --> 03:01.030
So my friends, the original data you have five rows in the output.

03:01.030 --> 03:02.550
We are using groupby like this.

03:02.550 --> 03:04.550
You will get only three rows.

03:04.550 --> 03:07.100
So this is exactly how the groupby group works.

03:07.100 --> 03:09.220
Let's go back to school and practice.

03:09.260 --> 03:09.500
Okay.

03:09.540 --> 03:10.700
So we have the following task.

03:10.700 --> 03:13.660
And it says find the total score for each country.

03:13.660 --> 03:18.980
So from reading this you can understand we have to do aggregations and we have to combine the data by

03:19.020 --> 03:19.500
a column.

03:19.500 --> 03:24.780
So now usually I start like this I start selecting the columns that I need in order to solve this task.

03:24.780 --> 03:25.460
So what do we need.

03:25.460 --> 03:30.260
We need the country and score from our table customers.

03:30.620 --> 03:31.700
So let's start like this.

03:31.700 --> 03:34.220
Now you can see we have the countries and the scores.

03:34.260 --> 03:37.420
And the task says we have to group up the data by the country.

03:37.420 --> 03:40.420
So that means this is the column where we're going to do the group by.

03:40.460 --> 03:42.700
And the total scores will be aggregated.

03:42.700 --> 03:47.140
So what we have to do, we're going to use the group by since it says for each country we're going to

03:47.140 --> 03:49.260
use it over here group by country.

03:49.260 --> 03:51.900
And now we have to go and aggregate the scores.

03:51.900 --> 03:53.140
We cannot leave it like this.

03:53.140 --> 03:55.460
So we're going to say the sum of the score.

03:55.460 --> 03:57.460
So let's go and execute it.

03:57.460 --> 04:02.060
And with that, as you can see we are getting the total scores for each country.

04:02.060 --> 04:06.460
So now instead of having five customers we have only three rows now.

04:06.500 --> 04:09.140
And that's because the countries has three rows.

04:09.180 --> 04:11.620
And now if you check the result, you can see something weird.

04:11.620 --> 04:13.100
It says no column name.

04:13.100 --> 04:15.740
And that's because we have changed the scores.

04:15.780 --> 04:17.780
It's not anymore the original score.

04:17.820 --> 04:18.500
It is.

04:18.660 --> 04:20.580
It is the total scores.

04:20.580 --> 04:22.340
We have summarized those values.

04:22.340 --> 04:24.540
So SQL don't know how we're going to call it.

04:24.540 --> 04:28.260
So those values doesn't come directly from the database.

04:28.300 --> 04:30.180
It is manipulation that you have done here.

04:30.220 --> 04:34.180
Now in order to give a nice name for that we can go and add aliases.

04:34.220 --> 04:34.820
An alias.

04:34.820 --> 04:37.940
It is only like a name that lives inside your query.

04:37.940 --> 04:43.340
So we can do it like this as and you can specify any name you want, like for example total score.

04:43.380 --> 04:47.140
And now SQL can understand okay this is the name for this column.

04:47.140 --> 04:50.700
And if you go and execute it you will see the new name in the results.

04:50.700 --> 04:53.820
But you have to understand this name exists only in this query.

04:53.860 --> 04:59.340
You are not renaming anything inside your database and you cannot use it in any other queries.

04:59.340 --> 05:04.060
It is just something that is known inside this query and only for your results.

05:04.100 --> 05:09.620
And of course you can rename anything any column like for example, here you can say this is the customer

05:10.020 --> 05:14.220
Country and if you excuse it, you are just renaming the column in the output.

05:14.260 --> 05:16.020
So this is really nice in SQL.

05:16.340 --> 05:16.620
Okay.

05:16.660 --> 05:19.020
So now there is one more thing about the groupby.

05:19.180 --> 05:25.100
The Non-aggregated columns that you are adding in the select must be as well mentioned in the groupby.

05:25.140 --> 05:29.820
So now for example, let's say that okay, I'm seeing now the countries the total scores I would like

05:29.820 --> 05:31.540
to see as well the first name.

05:31.540 --> 05:33.660
So you go over here and say you know what.

05:33.700 --> 05:34.980
Let's get the first name.

05:34.980 --> 05:38.020
So country first name the total scores and executes.

05:38.020 --> 05:40.420
You will get an error because SQL cannot tell you.

05:40.420 --> 05:45.380
I need only the columns that you want to group the data by or should be aggregated.

05:45.380 --> 05:50.260
So now the first name it is not aggregated and as well not used for the groupby.

05:50.300 --> 05:54.100
So it is just here to confuse SQL and it will not work.

05:54.100 --> 05:59.580
So if you bring a column, either it should be in the aggregation or it should be part of the groupby.

05:59.620 --> 06:04.380
So in order to fix this and you really want to see the first name, you can go over here and say, you

06:04.380 --> 06:07.660
know what, let's add it to the group by and execute.

06:07.660 --> 06:08.940
This time it's gonna work.

06:08.940 --> 06:13.810
Because all the columns that are mentioned here is as well part of the groupby.

06:13.850 --> 06:17.370
So now as you can see, we have the countries, the first name and the total scores.

06:17.410 --> 06:19.610
And you can see again we have five rows.

06:19.610 --> 06:20.890
We don't have three rows.

06:20.930 --> 06:25.850
And that's because now you are combining the data by the country and as well the first name.

06:25.890 --> 06:28.810
And now you can see in the output we are getting five rows.

06:28.850 --> 06:30.930
We are not getting any more than three rows.

06:30.970 --> 06:32.050
The three countries.

06:32.090 --> 06:37.810
And that's because SQL now grouping the data by two columns, a combination of the country and the first

06:37.810 --> 06:40.690
name and those two columns gives five combinations.

06:40.730 --> 06:42.650
And that means you will get five rows.

06:42.650 --> 06:47.810
So that means you have to be really careful what you are defining in the groupby and the number of the

06:47.810 --> 06:48.730
unique values.

06:48.730 --> 06:53.290
Thus those columns are generating can define the output, the results.

06:53.290 --> 06:58.610
So if you go and remove the first name, and from here as well, you are grouping by only one column.

06:58.610 --> 07:00.610
And this column has only three values.

07:00.610 --> 07:02.570
And that's why you are getting three rows.

07:02.610 --> 07:04.370
And with that of course we have solved the task.

07:04.410 --> 07:10.930
And now let's extend the task and say find the total score and total number of customers for each country.

07:10.930 --> 07:12.890
So that means we need two aggregations.

07:12.890 --> 07:17.440
We have the total score and as well we need the total number of customers.

07:17.440 --> 07:21.800
So from reading this you can understand we still want to group up the data by the country.

07:21.800 --> 07:24.560
But this time we need two types of aggregations.

07:24.560 --> 07:27.920
We need the total number of customers and the total scores.

07:27.920 --> 07:29.360
So we have almost everything.

07:29.360 --> 07:31.720
But what is missing is the second aggregation.

07:31.760 --> 07:36.760
Now what you can do, you can go over here and add another aggregate function called the count.

07:36.760 --> 07:39.480
And what we want to count is the number of customers.

07:39.480 --> 07:43.960
So we can go and add the ID over here and call it total customers.

07:43.960 --> 07:45.120
So now of course SQL.

07:45.440 --> 07:50.480
So now if you go and execute it you will get as well the total customers by their country.

07:50.520 --> 07:53.040
And now as you can see SQL has no problem with the ID.

07:53.080 --> 07:55.360
And that's because you are aggregating the ID.

07:55.360 --> 07:58.400
So SQL knows what to do with it and how to combine it.

07:58.400 --> 08:03.280
So that means you don't have to mention the ID and the country because you are aggregating it.

08:03.280 --> 08:04.040
So that's all.

08:04.040 --> 08:06.120
With that, we have solved as well the task.

08:06.160 --> 08:06.520
All right.

08:06.520 --> 08:10.200
So with that you have learned how to group up your data using the groupby.

08:10.240 --> 08:14.920
Next we are going to talk about another technique on how to filter your data, but this time using the

08:14.960 --> 08:16.160
having clause.

08:16.160 --> 08:16.920
So let's go.
